## Parallel Subagent Best Practices

**Sources**: [amux Orchestration Guide 2026](https://amux.io/guides/ai-agent-orchestration-2026/), [Agentic Engineering 2026](https://amux.io/guides/agentic-engineering/), [Subagents Deep Dive](https://ranjankumar.in/subagents-parallelism-inside-session), [STORM paper (arXiv 2605.20563)](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.20563v1), [Clarion AI Multi-Agent 2026](https://clarion.ai/insights-building-multi-agent-ai-systems-orchestration-memory-tool-use/), [Cursor Subagents Complete Guide](https://medium.com/@codeandbird/cursor-subagents-complete-guide-5853e8d39176)

### Coordination Pattern Selection

Choose the right orchestration pattern based on task structure:

```
Is the task naturally sequential?
├── Yes → Pipeline (stage N output feeds stage N+1)
└── No
    ├── Does task benefit from multiple perspectives?
    │   ├── Yes, 2 perspectives → Debate/Negotiator
    │   └── Yes, 3+ perspectives → Fan-out parallel
    └── No
        ├── Is task decomposable into clear subtasks?
        │   ├── Yes → Supervisor + Specialists (DEFAULT)
        │   └── No → Single agent (don't over-engineer)
        └── Massive scale (100+ tasks)? → Swarm (rare)
```

**Default: Supervisor + Specialists** — 96.3% success rate pattern. Start here. Add complexity only when task demands it.

#### Pattern Definitions

|| Pattern | When to Use | Example |
||---------|-------------|---------|
|| **Pipeline** | Sequential dependencies, each stage feeds next | Research → Draft → Review → Publish |
|| **Fan-out** | 3+ independent tasks, no shared state | Fix 3 separate test files in parallel |
|| **Supervisor + Specialists** | Decomposable with different expertise | Frontend dev + Backend dev + Security auditor |
|| **Debate** | Need multiple perspectives on same problem | Design decision with pros/cons analysis |

### When to Parallelize (Rule of 3)

Only dispatch parallel subagents when a task decomposes into **3+ independent, non-overlapping subtasks**. Below 3 tasks, a single agent or sequential dispatch is faster end-to-end.

Each parallel subtask should take an estimated **15–45 minutes**. Under 15 minutes, the overhead of a separate agent isn't worth it. Over 45 minutes, the task is probably decomposable further.

### Decomposition Criteria

Parallel tasks MUST meet ALL of these criteria:

| Criterion | Pass Check |
|-----------|------------|
| **Different files** | No two tasks touch the same file |
| **Different entities** | No two tasks touch the same function/class (semantic, not just file-level) |
| **Explicit interfaces** | When tasks must interact, the interface (URL, request/response shape) is pre-defined |
| **No shared state** | No task reads another's output to do its work |
| **Roughly equal cost** | No one subtask dominates the wall clock (slowest sets total time) |

**Semantic conflicts matter more than file conflicts**: Two agents editing different functions in the same file can produce a "clean" git merge that still breaks the build because of changed signatures, removed dependencies, or conflicting imports. Task decomposition must be at the function/entity level, not just file level.

### Clean Summary Discipline

Subagents return only **distilled results** (~400 tokens), not raw transcripts, full file contents, or test stdout. The orchestrator's context stays sharp.

```
✅ GOOD: "3 tests failed: auth.test.ts:42 (JWT expired), auth.test.ts:58 (null token). Fixed by adding null check."
❌ BAD: Full 20,000-token test output dumped into orchestrator context
```

### Scoped Context Injection

Task descriptions to subagents must be the **minimum needed** for that specific task. Do not dump the parent's full session context, rejected approaches, or unrelated decisions into the child's fresh context window.

```
✅ GOOD: "Add OAuth callback route at /api/auth/:platform/callback. See existing /api/auth/:platform/authorize for the pattern."
❌ BAD: [5,000-token dump of the entire auth architecture discussion, all rejected approaches, and 12 files of context]
```

### Least-Privilege Tool Access

Set `readonly` appropriately per subagent role:

| Role | readonly | Reason |
|------|----------|--------|
| Research/exploration | `true` | Cannot accidentally modify files while exploring |
| Review/audit | `true` | Read-only analysis, findings returned as summary |
| Implementation | `false` | Needs to write code |
| Test runner | `false` | Needs to execute test commands |

### Model Tiering by Complexity

| Task Type | Model | Signal |
|-----------|-------|--------|
| Mechanical (1–2 files, complete spec) | Fast/cheap model | Clear file paths, simple transformation |
| Integration (multi-file coordination) | Standard model | Cross-file imports, shared interfaces |
| Design/judgment (architecture decisions) | Most capable model | Requires broad codebase understanding |

### Post-Integration Verification

After all parallel subagents return:

1. **Check for conflicts**: Did agents edit the same file or function? If so, resolve before proceeding.
2. **Run full test suite**: Parallel agents that compile individually may produce a broken build when merged.
3. **Run full verification**: `pnpm run typecheck && pnpm run lint && pnpm run build`
4. **Verify semantic integrity**: A merge can be "clean" but still break logic — check that changed functions still compile together.

### Rate Limit Awareness

Too many concurrent subagent dispatches can hit API rate limits. When dispatching 5+ parallel subagents, stagger launches by 30–60 seconds.

### Timeout Specification

Every subagent dispatch MUST have an expected duration. Aggressive timeouts (2-5 minutes for bounded tasks) with explicit fallback behavior are mandatory.

|| Task Type | Timeout | Fallback |
||-----------|---------|----------|
|| Mechanical (1-2 files, complete spec) | 2 minutes | Retry with narrower scope |
|| Integration (multi-file coordination) | 5 minutes | Escalate to human if blocked |
|| Research/exploration | 5 minutes | Return partial findings, what was discovered |
|| Long-running background task | No timeout | Use `run_in_background: true` |

**Cascading timeout pattern**: If a subagent stalls, the orchestrator must have a recovery path — spawn a new subagent with narrower scope rather than waiting indefinitely.

### Circuit Breakers: Max Limits Per Request

Prevent runaway recursion and token burn:

|| Limit | Value | Rationale |
||-------|-------|-----------|
|| Max concurrent subagents | 5 | Rate limit awareness, manageable review load |
|| Max total per request tree | 10 | Prevent unbounded token consumption |
|| Max retries per failed subagent | 2 | Escalate instead of infinite retry loop |
|| Max nesting depth | 1 | Cursor model inheritance bugs at level 2+ |

### Background Async Pattern

For long-running tasks (research, large migrations, extensive refactoring), use background dispatch to keep the orchestrator interactive:

```typescript
Task({
  subagent_type: "generalPurpose",
  prompt: "[Task description with clear success criteria]",
  description: "Background research task",
  run_in_background: true  // Returns immediately, runs async
})
```

**After background dispatch**:
- Continue working on other tasks in the foreground
- Do NOT block waiting for the background task
- Check terminal output later or await completion notification
- Background subagents can run while foreground work continues

**Cursor 3.2 `/multitask`**: Use for queue parallelization and auto-decomposition. Each queued prompt becomes an async subagent. Works well for independent research tasks that don't need immediate results. Access via Agents Window.

### Anti-Patterns

| Anti-Pattern | Problem | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **Over-parallelization** | 2 tasks dispatched in parallel costs more than single agent | Use Rule of 3 |
| **Context pollution** | Subagent gets full parent context, stays polluted | Scope to minimum needed |
| **No review bottleneck planning** | 10 agents finish, human can only review 3 | Limit parallel dispatch to review capacity |
| **Force-push on shared branches** | Destroys other agents' work | Never force-push on branches others use |
| **Ignoring semantic collisions** | Two agents change same function signature | Decompose at entity level, not file level |
| **Dumping test output** | 20K tokens of test stdout floods parent context | Subagent processes, returns summary only |
| **Nested subagents** | Level 2+ falls back to Composer, unreliable behavior | Flatten to level 1 only |
| **CLI nesting** | CLI lacks Task tool interface for child spawning | Use IDE for any subagent work |
| **No timeout fallback** | Stalled subagent blocks entire workflow | Define recovery path before dispatch |
| **Blocking on background tasks** | Orchestrator stalls waiting for async work | Continue work, poll later |

